KaizoCore patch to libretro/melonDS 66b5d2634cd0a79030562811e6e05f5532f800ba (GPL-3.0, as melonDS is): melonDS's
own fix f143e89c931d12a234851e443b7d85f8017db9a3 ("fix UB in software renderer", Arisotura, 2025-09-29,
github.com/melonDS-emu/melonDS), brought back to this older source with the same changes.

The 3D software renderer's interpolator computes its perspective factor (yfactor) in SetX() only when the two W
values differ, but InterpolateZ() reads that factor for every W-buffered polygon. Across a span whose two ends have
the same W, the depth came from whatever the variable held before; for the per-scanline interpolator that is the
stack, so what got drawn depended on how the compiler laid the function out. Measured on the emulator, 2026-10-03,
running each game from boot with no input and a fixed clock: the libretro buildbot's binary of this commit (NDK r29)
and a build of the same source with NDK r26 drew 78 of the first 2,400 frames of HeartGold and 7 of Platinum's
differently on x86_64 (67 and 8 on arm64, run through the emulator's translation), a few pixels at a polygon edge
each time, and every one of those frames showed a picture drawn through this path. Filling uninitialized stack
variables with a pattern changed the picture again, the same whether it was done in every file or in
GPU3D_Soft.cpp alone. Black 2 and White 2 never went through it and drew the same in both.

The interpolator now knows it is W-buffering and computes the factor for it, as melonDS does since that fix, so the
core draws those pixels the same with any compiler. The sound was the same sample for sample in all four games,
on both ABIs.

Applied after 0001 by tools/cores/build_core.py melonds; libretrodroid/cores/melonds/PINNED.txt records the result.

